Current educational techniques in software testing emphasize thoroughness, neglecting testing efficiency. As a result, students spend excessive time on testing, disregarding its timeliness. We introduce inquiry-based conceptual questions as a novel method to promote efficient testing, prioritizing relevance over coverage. A study compared the effectiveness of this new approach with traditional evaluation methods. Surveys indicated significant improvements in testing speed without compromising conceptual coverage. Students also demonstrated enhanced testing skills and better understanding of testing concepts. Notably, incorporating frequent, timely feedback facilitated these outcomes. As an added benefit, this new method saved students' valuable time, allowing them to focus on key testing tasks. We empirically validated the benefits of inquiry-based conceptual questions for teaching software testing. 

