The empirical investigation presented in this study examined whether the newly introduced inquiry-based conceptual testing approach, which emphasizes testing relevant requirements with adequate coverage of key testing activities, could achieve better results than the established detailed evaluation method, which focuses on testing every requirement with high coverage of testing activities. The researchers compared the two methods using key performance indicators including testing speed, testing efficiency, and students' understanding of testing concepts. Notably, the newly introduced inquiry-based testing approach promoted testing speed and efficiency without compromising conceptual testing coverage. Moreover, the study revealed that incorporating frequent, timely feedback was essential for the success of the inquiry-based testing approach. As an added benefit, the new method allowed students to complete testing faster while still achieving high conceptual testing coverage and meeting the essential testing activities. The researchers validated the outcomes of the inquiry-based testing approach through an empirical investigation. 

