Achieving balance between utilization of sensitive data and protection of that data is crucial. One-sided differential privacy offers a potential solution by allowing utilization of data while still maintaining some level of privacy. However, research has revealed a significant shortcoming - any instance of confidentiality breach results in total loss of privacy. To address this issue, we introduce a more refined model, utilizing confidence intervals to measure privacy, and apply it to one-sided differential privacy. Within this framework, we develop methods for releasing statistical summaries with increased privacy resilience - meaning a confidentiality breach only slightly increases the risk of exposure. We also propose a metric to evaluate the tradeoff between privacy resilience and utilization of sensitive data. Moreover, depending on the nature of released information, we determine whether confidentiality breaches can be entirely prevented. Finally, we apply our developed framework to quantify privacy resilience in various statistical contexts. Our research demonstrates that, in certain situations, releasing sample mean and sample variance yields significantly improved privacy resilience when compared to releasing data itself. Notably, while confidentiality breaches are preventable in many instances, they remain unavoidable in some cases involving dependency of data. Ultimately, we disclose the limitations of our framework and suggest potential avenues for further research. Keywords: one-sided differential privacy, confidentiality breach, confidence intervals, privacy resilience. 

