This work focuses on recognizing handwritten mathematical expressions, with the data collected from students' interactions with mathematical symbols through sensor technology. Unlike conventional approaches, which rely on standardized handwriting, we've developed a novel approach for expressing mathematical symbols using binary sensor data. Additionally, we've created a dataset comprising sensor data from five different writing devices to facilitate reliable sensor-based ME recognition. Our proposed framework not only accommodates diverse writing styles but also identifies the physical characteristics of symbols, enabling ME recognition. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach is viable and compares favorably to the existing standard method, with further details available in the provided document. 

